{-
   A loop that counts and does nothing else, decided false at its header -
   `compiler/resolve_ssa/opt/loop.cpp`, and the five shapes that must keep theirs.

   Every claim here is a **number**. A loop removed that should have run answers with the value its
   accumulator started at, and a loop kept that should have gone is invisible in an answer - so each
   function below is weighted by a distinct prime in `main` and is called with arguments that make
   the loop run more than zero times. `.run.expect` and the optimized/unoptimized comparison the
   driver runs beside it are what turn a wrong removal into a failure; `.ssa.opt.expect` says which
   loops are actually still there.

   The loop this pass exists for is not in this file and cannot be: it is `Reclaim(Array(a))` in
   `prelude/Core/Array.yana`, one traversal over the live elements of an array whose element type
   has nothing to run - and a library body is left out of both dumps. `LiteralReclaim.yana`'s
   lowered golden is where it shows.

   The last two functions are not about loops at all. They are the two shapes phase 8 built the
   branch-implied facts for, measured, and did not keep - see `opt/README.md`'s note. They are here
   because what they record is a *structural* fact about this tier that a later change could undo:
   both need one SSA value to survive a block boundary, and at this tier every named value round
   trips through a slot, so the second test reads a second `load`. If cross-block promotion ever
   arrives here, this file's golden is what says so.
-}

data Held {id: Int}

let &live = 0 :: Int

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->self: Held):
        live = live - 1

fn held(id: Int) -> Held:
    live = live + 1
    return Held {id: id}

-- The plain case. The body computes a value nothing reads, out of a local the loop only reads and a
-- counter nothing outside it reads, so the whole loop goes and `k` is the answer.
fn counted(n: Int, k: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        let unused = k * i + 7
        i = i + 1

    return k

-- The body writes. `total` is a second store in the loop and the pass takes exactly one, which is
-- the counter's step - so this loop stays and its answer is the sum.
fn accumulates(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &total = 0
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        total = total + i
        i = i + 1

    return total

-- The counter is read afterwards. Removing the loop would leave `i` holding what it started at
-- rather than the bound, which is why the pass asks about the *allocation*'s use list and not only
-- about the values the loop defines.
fn survives(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        i = i + 1

    return i

{-
   A tracked value in the body.

   `held` is a call, so the purity clause refuses this loop before the ownership clause is reached -
   which is the same meeting point `IfConvert.yana`'s `chosen` records for if-conversion, and it is
   stated here rather than left to be discovered. Every way this stage produces an owner is a `call`
   or an `aggregate`, and the drop that ends one is not pure either, so the ownership clause has no
   case the purity clause has not already taken. It is here because "needs no rule of its own" and
   "never arrives" are different statements.
-}
fn owning(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        let doomed = held(i)
        i = i + 1

    return 0

-- Two ways out. A `break` gives the loop a second exit edge, so "the header's test is false" stops
-- being a complete description of not running it and the pass declines.
fn escapes(n: Int, stop: Int) -> Int:
    let &i = 0

    while i < n:
        if i == stop then break
        i = i + 1

    return i

-- Nested, with the inner one dead and the outer one not. The inner goes on the first round and the
-- outer keeps its own step, which is what the innermost-first order buys.
fn nested(rows: Int, columns: Int) -> Int:
    let &total = 0
    let &row = 0

    while row < rows:
        let &column = 0
        while column < columns:
            let unused = column + row
            column = column + 1

        total = total + row
        row = row + 1

    return total

{-
   The first shape phase 8 measured and did not keep: a zero test a dominating branch already
   decided.

   `d != 0` guards the division and `d == 0` inside the arm asks the same question again, which over
   in the shipping stage `foldProvenZeroTests` answers outright. Here the two tests are not tests of
   one value: `d` lives in a slot, `forwardMemory` is a per-block scan, and the guard's block and
   the arm's block therefore each hold their own `load`. Measured over the whole corpus, the walk
   asked 627 times and never once found a second zero test of the same SSA value in one body.

   Division needs no guard in this language - `doc/spec/types.md` makes it total and
   `makeDivisionTotal` at the lower tier implements it - so this shape is one a program has to be
   written to contain, which is the other half of why there is no pass for it.
-}
fn guarded(x: Int, d: Int) -> Int:
    if d != 0:
        let quotient = x / d
        if d == 0 then return -1
        return quotient

    return 0

{-
   The second: the sign/zero extension pair a checked subscript emits over there, which no subscript
   emits here.

   An index at this tier is a `Size` handed straight to `element.addr`, with the stride multiply the
   lowering's, and the bounds test `prelude/Core/Array.yana` writes is a `bitcast` to `USize` rather
   than a widening conversion - so a subscript produces no extension at all. Written out by hand, as
   below, the two extensions are still not of one value, because the unsigned one goes through a
   `bitcast` first. Measured over the whole corpus: no value is both sign-extended and zero-extended
   anywhere.
-}
fn widened(i: Int, n: UInt) -> Long:
    if (bitcast(i) :: UInt) < n:
        let wide = i :: Long
        let unsignedWide = (bitcast(i) :: UInt) :: ULong
        return wide + (bitcast(unsignedWide) :: Long)

    return 0

fn main() -> Int =
    counted(4, 3) * 2 + accumulates(5) * 3 + survives(6) * 5 + owning(4) * 7 +
    escapes(9, 4) * 11 + nested(3, 4) * 13 + guarded(84, 4) * 17 +
    (truncate(widened(6, 9)) :: Int) * 19 + live * 23
